  "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}\u2061(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
